.Dd September 22, 2026
.Dt SW 1
.Os PUtils
.Sh NAME
.Nm sw ,
.Nm switch
.Nd switch the terminal into another operating system's shell profile
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl l Ar dir
.Op Ar profile
.Op Fl c Ar command
.Nm
.Cm exit
.Nm
.Cm profile
.Cm add | rm | list | inspect | ins | default | set | build | help
.Op Ar args ...
.Nm
.Cm inspect | ins
.Ar profile
.Op Cm tool Op Cm add | rm | remove Ar tool ...
.Nm
.Cm os
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
opens a separate shell
.Em profile
that looks and behaves like another system while you keep working on your
own files.
Out of the box there is one profile,
.Cm debian13 ,
which gives you Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie) with its standard set of tools
and its standard shell,
.Xr bash 1 ,
so you can practise in the same environment as a university's Debian
machines.
.Pp
Inside a profile none of your own configuration applies: no
.Pa ~/.zshrc ,
no Homebrew, no packages you installed yourself.
You get only the tools of the chosen system plus the tools you add to that
profile, and the shell starts with that system's own defaults.
To go back, type
.Ic exit
or
.Ic sw exit .
.Pp
.Nm switch
is the same program under a second name.
.Ss How it works
.Bl -tag -width "macOS profiles"
.It Linux profiles
run the real distribution in a throw-away Docker container, so
.Xr uname 1 ,
.Pa /etc/os-release ,
the hostname, the GNU tools and the package names are genuinely those of
that system.
Your user name, uid and gid are the same as on the Mac.
.Pa /Users
and
.Pa /Volumes
are shared at the same paths, so
.Ev HOME
is your real home and the shell starts in the directory you were in.
Each profile has its own image,
.Sy sw- Ns Ar profile ,
built the first time you switch to it and rebuilt whenever its tools change.
Docker Desktop is started if it is not running.
.It macOS profiles
run on the Mac itself with a
.Ev PATH
of only
.Pa /usr/bin ,
.Pa /bin ,
.Pa /usr/sbin ,
.Pa /sbin
and the Homebrew formulae you share with the profile, and with the
system's standard shell,
.Xr zsh 1 .
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-c command"
.It Fl l Ar dir , Fl \-limit Ar dir
Limit the profile to
.Ar dir :
it becomes the home directory, so
.Ev HOME ,
.Ql ~
and a bare
.Ic cd
all point at it.
In a Linux profile
.Ar dir
is mounted as
.Pa /home/ Ns Ar user ,
like a university account, and nothing else of your file system is
visible.
If you start inside
.Ar dir ,
the shell starts at the same place inside the new home.
.Ar dir
may use
.Ql ~ ,
.Ql ~user ,
.Ql $VAR
and
.Ql ${VAR} ,
which
.Nm
expands itself, so they also work when quoted or stored in a profile.
It is created if it does not exist.
On a macOS profile only
.Ev HOME
and the starting directory change; the rest of the disk stays reachable.
.It Fl c Ar command
Run
.Ar command
with the profile's shell and exit, instead of starting an interactive shell.
.It Fl h , Fl \-help
Show a short help.
.It Fl V , Fl \-version
Show the version.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Nm Op Ar profile
Switch to
.Ar profile ,
or to the default profile when it is left out.
.It Nm Cm exit
Leave the profile.
It works only inside a profile, where it is the same as
.Ic exit .
.It Nm Cm profile add Ar name Oo Ar os Oc Oo Ar tool ... Oc Op Ar options
Create a profile called
.Ar name
(lowercase letters, digits,
.Ql \&. ,
.Ql _
and
.Ql - )
for the system
.Ar os
(see
.Nm Cm os ;
you are asked if it is left out) with the given extra
.Ar tool Ns s.
Options:
.Bl -tag -width "-H, --hostname name"
.It Fl s , Fl \-shell Ar shell
Run
.Ar shell
instead of the system's standard shell.
Add its package as a tool first.
.It Fl H , Fl \-hostname Ar name
The hostname the shell sees (Linux profiles).
.It Fl l , Fl \-home Ar dir
Always limit this profile to
.Ar dir ,
as if
.Fl l Ar dir
were given every time.
Variables are kept unexpanded and expanded at each switch.
.It Fl a , Fl \-arch Ar arch
.Cm amd64
or
.Cm arm64
(Linux profiles).
The default is the Mac's own architecture; use
.Cm amd64
to match x86 lab machines exactly, at some cost in speed.
.It Fl m , Fl \-mount Ar dir
Share
.Ar dir
with a Linux profile at the same path.
Repeatable; replaces the default
.Pa /Users
and
.Pa /Volumes .
.It Fl \-no-mounts
Share nothing except a
.Fl l
home.
.El
.It Nm Cm profile rm Ar name Op Fl f
Delete a profile together with its image, shell history and
.Pa custom.sh .
Asks first unless
.Fl f
is given.
The built-in
.Cm debian13
profile and the current default profile cannot be deleted.
.It Nm Cm profile list
List the profiles.
.Ql *
marks the default.
.It Nm Cm profile inspect Ar name , Nm Cm profile ins Ar name
The same as
.Nm Cm inspect Ar name .
.It Nm Cm profile default Op Ar name
Show the default profile, or make
.Ar name
the default.
.It Nm Cm profile set Ar name key Op Ar value
Change one setting of a profile:
.Cm os ,
.Cm shell ,
.Cm hostname ,
.Cm home ,
.Cm arch
or
.Cm mounts .
.Cm mounts
takes a colon-separated list of directories, or
.Cm none .
Leaving out
.Ar value
resets the setting to its default.
Changing
.Cm os
to a system with a different package manager drops the profile's tools.
.It Nm Cm profile build Ar name Op Fl \-pull
Rebuild a Linux profile's image now.
With
.Fl \-pull
the base image is updated first, which picks up the distribution's latest
updates.
.It Nm Cm profile help
Show help for the
.Cm profile
commands.
.It Nm Cm inspect Ar name , Nm Cm ins Ar name
Show everything about a profile: its system, shell, hostname, home, shared
directories, image state and tools.
.It Nm Cm inspect Ar name Cm tool
Show only the profile's tools: its base toolset, then one added tool per
line.
.It Nm Cm inspect Ar name Cm tool add Ar tool ...
Add tools to a profile.
For Linux profiles a tool is a package of that distribution
.Po
.Xr apt 8 ,
dnf, pacman or apk
.Pc ,
installed into the profile's image straight away; if a package cannot be
installed, the profile is left unchanged.
For macOS profiles a tool is an installed Homebrew formula; its programs
are shared with the profile.
.Cm @all
shares every Homebrew formula.
.It Nm Cm inspect Ar name Cm tool rm Ar tool ... , Nm Cm inspect Ar name Cm tool remove Ar tool ...
Remove tools from a profile.
.It Nm Cm os
List the systems a profile can be.
.El
.Sh SYSTEMS
.Bl -tag -width "ubuntu24.04" -compact
.It Cm debian13
Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie), bash.
Base toolset: every package of priority required, important and standard,
which is what the Debian installer's
.Dq standard system utilities
installs, including
.Xr man 1 ,
.Xr less 1 ,
.Xr nano 1 ,
.Xr vim 1
(vim.tiny) and bash completion.
.It Cm debian12
Debian GNU/Linux 12 (bookworm), same base toolset.
.It Cm ubuntu24.04 , Cm ubuntu22.04
Ubuntu LTS, bash; ubuntu-minimal and ubuntu-standard, unminimized.
.It Cm fedora
Fedora Linux, bash; the @core group with man pages.
.It Cm arch
Arch Linux (always amd64), bash; the base group with man pages.
.It Cm alpine
Alpine Linux, ash; BusyBox with man pages.
.It Cm macos
This Mac, zsh; only the macOS system tools.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/sw/config
Which profile is the default.
.It Pa ~/.config/sw/profiles/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa /profile
The profile's settings, one
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
per line;
.Cm mount
and
.Cm tool
may repeat.
.It Pa ~/.config/sw/profiles/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa /custom.sh
Your own settings for the profile (aliases, prompt, exports).
The profile's shell sources it at every switch; it is never overwritten.
.It Pa ~/.config/sw/profiles/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa /history
The profile's shell history, kept apart from your normal one.
.It Pa ~/.config/sw/profiles/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa /rc , .zshrc , .zshenv
Start-up files generated at every switch.
They load the system's own
.Pa /etc/profile
and
.Pa /etc/skel/.bashrc ,
then
.Pa custom.sh ,
and define
.Ic sw exit .
Inside a Linux profile this directory is
.Pa /var/lib/sw .
.It Pa ~/.config/sw/profiles/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa /Dockerfile
The recipe of the profile's image.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XDG_CONFIG_HOME
.It Ev SW_HOME
Use this directory instead of
.Pa ~/.config/sw .
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set,
.Nm
keeps its files in
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sw .
.It Ev SW_PROFILE , Ev SW_OS
Set inside a profile to its name and system.
.Nm
refuses to switch again while
.Ev SW_PROFILE
is set.
.El
.Pp
Inside a profile the environment is rebuilt from scratch: only
.Ev HOME ,
.Ev USER ,
.Ev LOGNAME ,
.Ev SHELL ,
.Ev TERM
and the time zone come from the Mac, and
.Ev LANG
is
.Ql C.UTF-8
on Linux.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with the status of the profile's shell or
.Ar command ,
or 1 if it cannot switch.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Switch to Debian 13 in the current directory, then leave again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sw
you@debian:~/Desktop$ gcc --version
bash: gcc: command not found
you@debian:~/Desktop$ exit
.Ed
.Pp
Give the Debian profile the tools a C course needs:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sw inspect debian13 tool add gcc make gdb valgrind
$ sw inspect debian13 tool
.Ed
.Pp
Practise in a home directory of its own, as on a lab machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sw -l ~/uni/home
$ sw -l '$HOME/uni/home' debian13
.Ed
.Pp
A profile shaped like the lab machines, always limited to one home:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sw profile add lab debian13 gcc make git -H lab-pc01 -a amd64 \e
      -l '$HOME/uni/home'
$ sw lab
.Ed
.Pp
A clean macOS shell that only has git from Homebrew:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sw profile add mac macos git
$ sw mac
.Ed
.Pp
Run a single command on Ubuntu without staying there:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sw profile add ubuntu ubuntu24.04
$ sw ubuntu -c 'lsb_release -a'
.Ed
.Sh CAVEATS
A Linux profile is a container, not a virtual machine: the kernel is Docker
Desktop's Linux kernel, you are not root, services such as systemd do not
run, and anything installed from inside the shell is lost at
.Ic exit .
Add tools with
.Nm Cm inspect Ar name Cm tool add
instead.
.Pp
Only directories Docker Desktop shares (by default
.Pa /Users ,
.Pa /Volumes ,
.Pa /private
and
.Pa /tmp )
can be mounted into a Linux profile.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bash 1 ,
.Xr zsh 1 ,
.Xr docker 1 ,
.Xr brew 1
